.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt RACH2-INSTALL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rach2-install
.Nd install Rach2 on a disk, update an installed root, make room for the data partition or move a disk to larger kernel slots
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -source Ar DIR
.Nm
.Op Fl -console Cm term
.Op Fl -i915
.Fl -update Ar IMAGE DISK
.Nm
.Fl -data Ar DISK SIZE
.Nm
.Fl -slots Ar DISK
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
command installs Rach2 on a disk of the running machine.
It runs from the recovery system on the install stick.
The stick contains a whole disk image and a manifest giving its name,
length, and SHA-256.
Its exFAT volume also contains
.Pa rach2-metal-disk.ext2
for root updates, GRUB's EFI image
.Pa bootx64.efi
with its digest in
.Pa bootx64.efi.sha256 ,
which an update takes to convert an MBR disk, and room beside the images
for saved copies of the 64 MiB boot and recovery partitions.
.Pp
The disk image has a GUID partition table: partition 1 is the boot
partition, the EFI system partition, with GRUB's EFI image as
.Pa \eEFI\eBOOT\eBOOTX64.EFI
beside the kernels, the menu and its environment block; partition 2 is
the recovery partition, partition 3 the ext2 root, and partition 4 the
BIOS boot partition, which holds GRUB's BIOS core.
The installer adds partition 6, the data partition, after the root, as
.Xr disk 7
describes.
The disk boots by BIOS and by UEFI.
Its menu boots the saved default, the candidate kernel once committed,
and falls back to the previous kernel after a candidate boot that never
reached user space, as Ubuntu's
.Ql recordfail
does: the candidate's entries set
.Ql recordfail=1
in the environment block before they load the kernel,
.Xr init 8
clears it with
.Ql bootctl booted
once user space runs, and while it stays set the menu boots the fallback.
A committed kernel that panics at boot therefore resets once, after the
panic's 30 seconds, into the fallback, and not into itself for ever.
The menu of a disk installed before this version lacks these lines, and
.Fl -update
adds them, as below.
On the installed machine,
.Ql bootctl grub
replaces the BIOS core in the BIOS boot partition; on a disk installed
with an MBR, which
.Fl -update
keeps, it replaces the core in the gap after the master boot record.
Either way it writes the one of two tails the running core does not use
and then selects it, and sector 0 is never written; see
.Xr bootctl 8 .
UEFI firmware must have Secure Boot turned off, since the pinned GRUB
image is not signed.
The manifest identifies the stick, regardless of its position among
the machine's disks.
The
.Fl -source Ar DIR
option reads the image and manifest from an already mounted directory.
.Pp
One run works at a time: each takes an exclusive
.Xr flock 2
lock on
.Pa /tmp/rach2-install.lock
before it looks at a disk, and a second run started meanwhile stops at once
and says so.
The lock goes with the run however it ends.
.Pp
All questions precede the first write.
The final question confirms the disk to overwrite.
Invalid disk, password, account name, key-file and recovery-key answers
are explained and requested again.
Standard input that ends before the last answer stops the run, and
nothing is written.
The questions are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Which disk
A disk is offered if it is fixed, has no mounted partitions, uses
512-byte sectors, is not the source stick, and is at least as large
as the image.
Eligible disks are numbered.
Other disks follow, each with the reason it was excluded.
Enter a number from the list or the corresponding node,
.Pa /dev/diskN .
.It Root's password
Enter the password twice, with terminal echo disabled.
An empty password or a pair that differs is requested again.
.It First account name
The name must start with a lowercase letter and contain only lowercase
letters, digits, hyphens, and underscores.
It may contain at most 32 characters and may not be
.Ql root .
An invalid name is reported with the rule it violates.
.It Account password
Enter it twice, as for root.
.It Account keys
Key files found on the stick and in the recovery system are listed.
Enter a listed number, another file's path, or an empty answer for no keys.
A path that is not a file is requested again.
The selected file is copied to the account's
.Pa ~/.ssh/authorized_keys .
.It Recovery key
A public key for the recovery system on the disk, offered as the account
keys are, or an empty answer for none.
The recovery system starts an SSH server only where it has a key, and a
root update runs from it, so this key lets a machine installed from a
stick with no key be updated over the network.
A file larger than 16384 bytes, one that is not text, one that holds a
private key, and one with no line that is a key are refused and the
question is asked again.
.It Root size
Give the size the root grows to, a number followed by
.Ql M ,
.Ql G
or
.Ql T
for mebibytes, gibibytes or tebibytes; the rest of the disk becomes the
data partition.
An empty answer is
.Ql 64G .
The answer
.Ql whole ,
.Ql y
or
.Ql yes
grows the root into the whole disk and makes no data partition, and
.Ql n
or
.Ql no
leaves the root at the image's size.
Any other answer is explained and requested again.
A size smaller than the image's root leaves the root at the image's size.
.It Confirmation
The disk's identity, account, keys, recovery key, and choice of root size
are displayed.
Type the disk's node name to proceed.
The words
.Ql stop ,
.Ql STOP ,
.Ql Stop ,
.Ql quit
and
.Ql q
end the run without writing.
An invalid answer repeats the confirmation question without discarding
the earlier answers.
.El
.Pp
After confirmation, the installer performs these steps in order:
.Bl -enum
.It
Read the source image and compare its SHA-256 with the manifest.
A mismatch stops the install before the disk is written.
.It
Write the image with
.Ql reflash -p ,
which reports progress every sixteen mebibytes.
.It
Read the written range back and compare its hash with the image's hash.
.It
Move the GUID table's backup to the disk's last sectors with
.Ql diskctl relocate ,
which also reloads the partition table, since the image's backup is
where the image ended and a reader looks for it at the disk's end.
An image with an MBR is reloaded with
.Ql diskctl reread .
.It
Write
.Pa /etc/passwd ,
.Pa /etc/group ,
and
.Pa /etc/shadow
into the ext2 root.
Each file is written under a separate name and renamed into place.
The image's system accounts, those with an ID below 1000 and
.Ql nobody
and
.Ql nogroup
of ID 65534, whose name and ID the new files do not have, are appended
to them with their groups and shadow lines, so a program of the image
keeps its account, as the GNU system's OpenSSH server keeps
.Ql sshd ,
and a system account the files have by the image's name and ID with no
shadow line gets the image's, which
.Xr pwck 8
asks for.
Create the account's home directory and install its keys.
.It
Write
.Ql root-password-login yes
to
.Pa /etc/rach2/sshd.conf .
This makes init start Dropbear without
.Fl g ,
permitting root to log in over SSH with a password.
.It
Make the data partition, when a size was given and the disk has 131072
sectors, 64 MiB, or more after the root's room up to its backup table;
a disk with fewer, or with an MBR, has none, and the installer says so.
.Ql diskctl data
makes entry 6 from the first mebibyte boundary after the root's room,
and
.Xr mke2fs 8
makes its file system, ext2 with an inode for every 64 KiB, labelled
.Ql Rach2 data .
.It
Grow the root, if requested, with
.Xr growroot 8
and the disk's identity
.Ql diskctl id
prints, as
.Ql GPTID=GUID,3 :
up to the data partition when there is one, and into the rest of the
disk otherwise.
.El
.Pp
The recovery partition is the FAT32 volume among the first four partitions
that holds
.Pa /boot/recovery ;
each is looked at mounted read-only, since a writable mount of a FAT
volume left dirty repairs it, and only the one found is mounted again to
be written.
If the installer finds a recovery partition, it archives the three account
files, the SSH policy,
.Pa /etc/vconsole.conf ,
the saved networks in
.Pa /etc/wpa_supplicant/wlan0.conf ,
.Pa /etc/machine-id ,
and from
.Pa /var/lib
the sound and backlight levels and the IPv6 secret, where the root has them,
under the same paths in
.Pa rach2-config-a .
Dropbear's host keys go in
.Pa rach2-config-a/etc/dropbear ,
and OpenSSH's, which a GNU root's server has, in
.Pa rach2-config-a/etc/ssh .
For each account in passwd, the tree of its home directory's
.Pa .ssh
goes in the tar file
.Pa rach2-config-a/keys/UID.tar ,
including root's keys in
.Pa keys/0.tar .
The home is the passwd home field, which need not match the account name.
For a shared UID, only the first passwd entry supplies keys.
Numeric file names keep accounts whose names differ only in case
separate on FAT32, and the tar file keeps the names, symbolic links and
hard links of the tree, which FAT32 would fold to one case, match to a short
alias or refuse.
The tree is copied first and each symbolic link in it made to lead where it
led in the root: one into the tree is written relative to its place, one to
a regular file elsewhere is replaced by a copy of that file with its owner
and mode, a later link to the same file, by name or by a hard link, becoming
a link to that copy, and any other is written as the absolute path it led
to.
A link to a file on a path through a directory that the account may not
search stops the update before the root is written, since the copy would
let the account read that file.
A restore reads
.Pa keys/UID
as a directory when an archive written by an older version has one.
A home is read inside the root it belongs to, as a process whose root
directory is that root would find it: each symbolic link along it is
followed within the root, and
.Pa ..
stops at the root, so a home such as
.Pa /../../mnt/other
is that root's
.Pa /mnt/other
and never a directory outside the disk.
A home that leads through more than 40 links stops the update before the
root is written.
Normal updates preserve .ssh in every passwd home, including homes outside
.Pa /home ,
with their owners and modes.
Each kept tree, and each symbolic link followed to reach it, goes back at
the path it had in the old root, replacing what the image has at that path
and any symbolic link of the image above it, so that every home and .ssh of
passwd leads where it led before the update.
Each symbolic link inside a kept .ssh is made to lead where it led in the
old root: one into the same .ssh is written relative to its place, one into
a kept tree or link as the absolute path it led to, and one to a regular
file elsewhere is replaced by a copy of that file with its owner and mode, a
later link to the same file becoming a link to that copy, unless an account
whose .ssh it is may not search a directory on the way to that file.
A link that leads to a directory, a device or nothing that is not kept
stops the update before the root is written, since the update would lose
what it leads to or let it lead to the image's file of that name.
After the kept trees are back, the image's .ssh is removed from
.Pa /root
and from every account home, each read inside the new root, wherever it is
not one of the kept trees, including accounts with no archived keys.
An image's .ssh that is a symbolic link is removed as the link, never what
it leads to, and one that holds a kept tree is left and stops the update.
An update stops before anything is written when an account's home is, or
lies inside, the .ssh of another account, since that .ssh put back whole
would replace the home.
Passwords are stored as hashes.
The archive preserves accounts and keys for an update when the root cannot
be read.
.Pp
The recovery key, when one was given, is copied to
.Pa boot/recovery_authorized_keys
on the recovery partition; with none, a file the image carried there is
removed.
The recovery system's init reads that file when it boots from the
partition, which the disk's recovery entries mark with
.Ql rach2.slot=recovery ,
and adds its keys to root's; the install stick's recovery system does not
read it.
An update leaves the file as it is.
The installed root's demonstration accounts and keys are removed.
The recovery partition keeps its own accounts and keys so that it can
be used when the installed root will not boot.
.Pp
With
.Fl -update Ar IMAGE DISK ,
.Nm
writes a root file system image into an installed machine's root partition.
The partition table is changed only to grow that partition into the
free space after it.
The data partition is not moved, and the files on it are kept; the
update writes only its directory
.Pa rach2-update-keep ,
as below.
The disk must have a first partition that holds this system's boot files
and an installed root containing
.Pa /etc/rach2 .
Both are proved by reads alone, with every partition mounted read-only,
before any partition of the disk is mounted writable, since a writable
mount of a FAT volume left dirty repairs it.
The boot partition must hold the 1024-byte environment block, a menu with
entries for both kernel slots, and one slot or both;
.Ql bootctl status
alone is no proof, since it reads neither the kernels nor the menu.
A boot partition that a stop in its conversion left unreadable is
accepted as described below, and a root that does not read is accepted
when the recovery partition holds the recovery kernel and a whole archive
of the configuration.
Then what a stop left of a slot's growth, of the menu's replacement or of
GRUB's EFI image's is finished, as
.Fl -slots
finishes it, below.
.Pp
The optional
.Fl -console Cm term
sets the installed root's candidate, fallback and text boot lines in
.Pa /boot/grub/grub.cfg
on partition 1 to carry
.Ql rach2.console=term
exactly once, replacing any previous console setting on those lines.
The optional
.Fl -i915
sets the same lines to carry
.Ql rach2.i915
exactly once, which binds the Intel graphics driver to the machine's chip
.Pq Xr i915 4 .
Recovery lines are unchanged.
The new menu is replaced as described under
.Sx Replacing a boot file ,
then the partition is unmounted and checked with
.Ql bootctl --target DISKp1 status
before the trial is armed.
A failed write or check stops the update.
Repeating either option leaves the same menu; without either the update
leaves the boot lines as they were.
Both options require
.Fl -update ,
and
.Fl -console
accepts only
.Cm term .
.Pp
Before replacing a readable root, the installer copies its account files,
SSH policy,
.Pa /etc/vconsole.conf ,
saved Wi-Fi networks, machine identifiers, kept sound and backlight levels,
server host keys, account keys, including root's keys, and the home
directories:
.Pa /home ,
root's home
.Pa /root ,
every passwd home under
.Pa /home ,
and the directory each symbolic link at the top of
.Pa /home
leads to.
Each is read inside the root as a home is and kept at the path it leads to,
with the symbolic links followed on the way, and so is the .ssh of every
account, wherever it lies.
An update stops before anything is written when one of these, or a link on
the way to one, is the root itself, one of
.Pa /bin , /boot , /dev , /etc , /lib , /lib32 , /lib64 , /libx32 , /proc , /run ,
.Pa /sbin , /sys , /tmp , /usr
or
.Pa /var ,
which the image replaces, or a name the copy uses for itself; a link at the
top of
.Pa /home
that leads to one of them is kept as the link alone.
It keeps them in
.Pa /dev/shm ,
the recovery system's tmpfs of half the machine's memory; an update whose
homes do not fit there stops before the root is written.
Since a power cut loses that copy, the whole of it also goes, as one tar
file
.Pa rach2-update-keep/keep.tar ,
to a volume that outlives one: the data partition, entry 6 of a GUID table
with an ext2 volume labelled
.Ql Rach2 data ,
or, when it is not there or has no room, the recovery partition, when the
file fits there and is under 4 GiB.
The file is synced, its volume unmounted and mounted again, and it is
hashed again from the disk; only when that SHA-256 is the one written does
.Pa keep.sha256
go beside it, and the archive names the partition and the hash in its file
.Pa update-keep .
A copy that does not read whole, either time, stops the update before
anything is destroyed.
An update with no room for the file on either volume stops before anything
is destroyed.
The copies are removed when the update finishes.
An update that stops after it began to write the root leaves them, and an
update of the same disk run again takes the copy in
.Pa /dev/shm
in the same boot, or after a reboot the file the marked archive names, once
its hash is proved, in place of the root and the archive, so that the homes
are kept; one whose file does not prove whole stops.
An update of another disk is refused while the copy in
.Pa /dev/shm
is there.
These are restored after the write, in place of the image's, preserving
passwords changed since installation, the console's keymap and the logins
kept in the homes, and the image's system accounts the restored files lack are
appended to them, as for an install.
The absence of account keys is preserved too.
If the root cannot be read, the account files, policy and keys are taken
from the recovery partition's archive and the root partition is replaced:
the partition the archive names in its file
.Pa root ,
.Ql ENTRY START ,
the entry and its first sector, which the update refuses when the table's
entry of that number begins elsewhere.
For an archive written before it named the root, the partition is entry 3
of a GUID table, or the one entry of type 0x83 of an MBR; an MBR with more
than one is refused.
An update also stops when the archive names one partition and another
reads as the root.
When the root and archive can both be read but differ, each differing
configuration file is reported and the root's copy is kept.
.Pp
Before anything is destroyed the archive is refreshed from the retained
configuration, with
.Pa root
and
.Pa update-keep ,
and read back whole.
It is marked as an update in progress, by a file
.Pa update-in-progress
in the current slot, only once the boot partition is ready and just before
the raw write of the root begins, so that an update stopped by a failed
check or a power cut before then leaves an archive that sends the next
update to the root, which is still whole.
An update whose root already holds the image's accounts, or does not read,
or whose write began, writes the archive marked.
The archive has two slots,
.Pa rach2-config-a
and
.Pa rach2-config-b .
A slot is complete only when it contains all three account files and its
.Pa generation
file contains a decimal integer from 1 to 2147483647.
A slot with a missing or invalid generation is ignored.
Complete legacy archives count as generation 0; any generation file in
one is ignored.
Readers choose the highest generation, breaking ties in this order:
.Pa rach2-config ,
.Pa rach2-config.new ,
.Pa rach2-config.old ,
.Pa rach2-config-a ,
then
.Pa rach2-config-b .
The writer rebuilds B when A was chosen, and A otherwise.
It removes the destination's generation file, syncs, removes the destination,
writes its contents, syncs, writes the next generation, and syncs again.
Only then does it remove all three legacy names, which it never writes.
An install leaves A at generation 1.
Before writing an updated root, the installer writes B, first writing A
if needed.
After restoring the accounts it writes A without the mark, so A is the
resting slot after a complete run.
A counter at 2147483647 stops further archive writes.
An update interrupted after writing the image and before restoring the
accounts leaves the image's accounts on the root.
The next update, when the copy in
.Pa /dev/shm
is gone, uses the file the marked archive names; when an archive marked by
an earlier version names none, it uses the marked archive, preserving the
server's identity and the accounts' keys and not the other home files.
Each archived account receives its UID's keys in the home from passwd.
A tree from
.Pa keys/UID.tar
keeps the numeric owners and modes it was archived with, so a file of
root's or of another account that it holds stays that owner's; its symbolic
links are root's.
A key directory of an older archive, which records no owners, receives the
account's uid and gid, mode 700 on its directories and 600 on its files,
and a symbolic link keeps its own mode.
The whole path of an account's .ssh is read inside the copy, so a symbolic
link restored with another account's keys is followed inside the copy and
never out of it; each key tree is unpacked into a private directory and
replaces what is at that path only when whole.
An update stops before the root is written when such a path is one the
copy may not keep, as above.
Accounts of different UIDs whose .ssh is one directory have it restored
once, from the first of them in passwd; when their archived copies differ,
the update stops before the root is written.
The keys go back at the paths that archived passwd names, whatever
symbolic links the image has there.
The server's host keys receive owner 0:0.
A missing home is created with mode 700 and the same owner.
Keys for a UID absent from the archived passwd are ignored.
Other home files are not archived.
An older archive without UID key directories cannot restore account keys.
The recovery partition is mounted so that only root can read the archive.
The image's home directories and their keys are removed whether or not
the old homes were retained.
.Pp
The image is checked before anything is written.
Its length must be a whole number of sectors and fit the partition.
It must begin with an ext2 file system.
It must read whole, and if
.Pa IMAGE.sha256
exists, its SHA-256 must match it.
.Pp
An update requires the disk's recovery partition, and proves before
anything is destroyed that the recovery system there would start: its
kernel
.Pa boot/recovery
carries a Multiboot2 header and reads whole, its RAM image
.Pa boot/recovery.krffs
reads whole and begins with the root node of a KRFFS image, which the
kernel mounts, its own menu
.Pa boot/grub/recovery.cfg
loads both, the partition carries the FAT label
.Ql RACH2RECOV
in its boot sector and its root directory, by which GRUB finds that menu
when the boot partition does not read, and the boot partition's entry
.Ql recovery
loads both from the volume with the recovery partition's FAT serial.
A disk that fails any of these is not updated.
.Pp
Before writing, the installer sets the saved boot entry to
.Ql recovery
and cancels any armed trial.
.Pp
An MBR disk, the layout disks installed before Rach2 booted by UEFI have,
is then converted to boot by UEFI as well, when
.Pa bootx64.efi
is beside
.Ar IMAGE ,
as the install stick has it.
The disk keeps its MBR.
If
.Pa bootx64.efi.sha256
is there, the EFI image's SHA-256 must match it.
UEFI firmware reads a FAT32 volume only when it has 65525 clusters or more,
and the 64 MiB boot partition such a disk has, at eight sectors to a
cluster, has 16348; its first sector and its files are copied to
.Pa rach2-boot-keep
on the recovery partition, compared whole with the partition once synced,
and marked whole by a file
.Pa complete
written last; its file system is made again with
.Ql mkfs.vfat ,
which takes one sector to a cluster, its serial, by which the menu finds
it, is written back into the new boot sector and its backup, and the
files are copied back.
The environment block, whose saved default is
.Ql recovery
by then, and the kernels go back first and are synced and compared, and
the menu goes back last, through a copy renamed to its name, so that a
stop before it leaves a partition with no menu, from which GRUB starts the
recovery system.
The copy is removed once the volume, mounted again read-only, holds every
kept file whole and
.Xr bootctl 8
reads it.
A boot partition of less than 33 MiB holds no such volume, and its disk is
left to boot by BIOS alone, as one is when
.Pa bootx64.efi
is not beside the image, when the disk has no recovery partition, or when
the recovery partition has no room for the copy.
GRUB's EFI image is put on the boot partition as
.Pa \eEFI\eBOOT\eBOOTX64.EFI ,
replaced as described under
.Sx Replacing a boot file .
The image is left alone when the partition has it already, as it does at
every update after the first, since the disk keeps its MBR.
Its prefix opens the menu the BIOS core opens, and
.Ql diskctl esp
gives the partition the EFI system partition's type, 0xEF.
A stop while the file system is made again leaves a boot partition the
BIOS core reads no menu from; it then starts the recovery system from
partition 2, and the update run again there finds the copy marked whole,
which holds both slots, the environment block and the menu, and so shows
the disk is this system's, makes the file system again from it and goes
on.
The kernels in the disk's slots must be of this version or later for
.Ql bootctl commit
to work after the conversion, since an older kernel publishes no
.Pa /dev/bootpart
for a boot partition of type 0xEF.
.Pp
A kernel slot,
.Pa /boot/kernel
or
.Pa /boot/fallback ,
smaller than the slots on the disks this version makes, 16 MiB, the size
.Ql bootctl status
prints, is then grown to it as
.Fl -slots
grows it, below, since
.Xr bootctl 8
rewrites a slot in place, never grows it, and refuses a kernel in a
smaller one.
A boot partition with no room for the copy keeps its slot, with a note.
.Pp
A menu in
.Pa /boot/grub/grub.cfg
on partition 1 that lacks the recordfail lines described above, the menu
of a disk installed before this version, is given them: each entry that
boots the candidate slot sets
.Ql recordfail=1
and saves it before it loads the kernel, and the test that boots the
fallback while it stays set follows the saved default's candidate line.
The new menu is replaced as described under
.Sx Replacing a boot file ,
and checked with
.Ql bootctl --target DISKp1 status
before the trial is armed.
A menu that has the test is left as it is, and so, with a note, is one
with no candidate entry or no such line.
.Pp
Both kernel entries boot the same root; recovery runs from RAM.
After the update, the new root is armed for one trial boot.
A failed trial or an interrupted write returns the machine to recovery.
After trying the new root,
.Ql bootctl commit
makes it the saved default.
.Pp
With
.Fl -data Ar DISK SIZE ,
.Nm
makes room for the data partition on a disk installed without one, whose
root fills the space up to the next partition.
It runs from the recovery system, with nothing on
.Ar DISK
mounted.
The disk must have a GUID table with entry 6 free.
The root is the highest of entries 4 to 1 that mounts as ext2 and has
.Pa /etc/passwd
and
.Pa /etc/rach2 ;
.Ar SIZE ,
in the form the root size question takes, must not exceed it.
The installer checks the root with
.Ql e2fsck -fp ,
which must end with status 0 or 1, shrinks its file system to
.Ar SIZE
with
.Xr resize2fs 8 ,
gives its entry that length with
.Ql diskctl shrink ,
and makes the data partition over the sectors freed, up to the next
partition, as an install makes it.
A run stopped after any step can be run again: the file system already
at
.Ar SIZE
is not moved, an entry already that length is not written, and an entry 6
named
.Ql Rach2 data
whose file system checks is kept.
.Pp
With
.Fl -slots Ar DISK ,
.Nm
moves a disk installed before the kernel slots grew to the slots of this
version, from the recovery system, with partition 1 of
.Ar DISK
not mounted;
.Ql bootctl kernel
and
.Ql bootctl fallback
refuse its smaller slots.
A disk whose slots have the size already is left as it is.
A trial armed in the environment block is refused, since GRUB would take
it at a stop.
The saved default is set to
.Ql recovery ,
which GRUB starts from partition 2 whatever the boot partition holds.
Then each slot, the candidate first and the fallback after it, is copied
beside itself as
.Pa kernel.new
or
.Pa fallback.new ,
padded with zeros to the new size and compared with the slot and its
padding; only then is the slot removed and the copy renamed to its name,
which rewrites the copy's directory entries in place.
The other slot is whole throughout, and the slots' kernels are the same
bytes as before.
Once both are grown and
.Xr bootctl 8
reads the volume, the saved default is put back:
.Ql candidate
if it was, and
.Ql fallback
for any other value but
.Ql recovery ,
which the menu treats alike.
A stop part way leaves a machine that starts the recovery system.
The command run again there finishes what the stop left: a copy with no
slot beside it, cut off before its rename, takes the slot's name; a copy
beside its slot is unfinished and is removed; and a slot missing with no
copy is made again from the other slot, copied as
.Pa kernel.tmp
or
.Pa fallback.tmp ,
compared with the other slot and synced before it takes the slot's name,
so that a stop leaves no part of a kernel under the slot's name.
Both names are 8.3 names, so the rename rewrites one directory entry in
place.
A menu or EFI image whose name a stop took while it was replaced is put
back from its kept copy first.
.Ql bootctl status
refuses a boot partition that lacks a slot, one where a stop while a copy
was padded left both slots and a copy whose cluster chain is longer than
its size, and one whose two FATs a stop left disagreeing, which a writable
mount repairs; and it reads neither the kernels nor the menu.
So nothing is changed on any disk until reads alone, with every partition
mounted read-only, show it is this system's: the boot partition holds the
environment block, a menu with entries for both slots, and one slot or
both, and a root carries
.Pa /etc/rach2
or the recovery partition holds the recovery kernel and a whole archive of
the configuration.
An update finishes such a partition in the same way before it goes on.
It finds the saved default at
.Ql recovery
and leaves it there, saying which command sets it.
Growing the fallback takes the most room, two new slots and the old
fallback: 36 MiB on a disk with 4 MiB slots, which the 64 MiB boot
partition of an installed disk has.
.Ss Replacing a boot file
The menu,
.Pa /boot/grub/grub.cfg ,
is replaced whole on the boot partition.
FAT32 renames over a name by freeing the old file's directory entries and
the new one's before it writes the name again, so a stop between those
writes leaves neither name.
So the new bytes are written beside the file as
.Pa FILE.new ;
a copy of them,
.Pa FILE.keep ,
is synced and compared before
.Pa FILE.new
is renamed over the file, and is removed only once the file has its name
again.
A stop leaves the old file or the new one whole under its name, or no file
and a whole
.Pa FILE.keep ,
which the next update or
.Fl -slots
puts back before it changes anything else; the menu is read from
.Pa grub.cfg.keep
meanwhile when the disk is proved this system's.
GRUB, started by BIOS or by the EFI image, opens the recovery partition's
menu when the boot partition has none.
.Pp
GRUB's EFI image,
.Pa \eEFI\eBOOT\eBOOTX64.EFI ,
is the one name UEFI firmware finds a loader by on an MBR disk, so it never
loses that name.
The new image is written beside it as
.Pa BOOTX64.TMP ,
synced and compared.
With no image there yet,
.Pa BOOTX64.TMP
is renamed, which rewrites its one directory entry in place.
With one, the partition is unmounted and the first clusters and sizes of
the two files' directory entries are swapped in one write of the sector
that holds both, so that the name moves from the old image to the new one
at once, and the old image, then
.Pa BOOTX64.TMP ,
is removed.
A stop leaves
.Pa BOOTX64.EFI
whole, with the old image or the new one, and perhaps a
.Pa BOOTX64.TMP ,
which the next update or
.Fl -slots
removes.
When the two entries are in different sectors, as only other files in
.Pa \eEFI\eBOOT
can make them, the update stops with the old image in place.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The exit status is 0 when the disk contains the installed system,
for an update, when the new root is on disk and armed for a trial boot,
with
.Fl -data ,
when the data partition is on the disk, and with
.Fl -slots ,
when both slots have this version's size and the saved default is back.
Other statuses indicate failure, with the reason on standard error.
A failure before the image write stops the run without starting that write.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Boot the install stick.
Its recovery system starts a root shell on the console with no login;
there, run:
.Dl rach2-install
.Pp
From recovery, identify the target disk and the stick in
.Pa /proc/disk .
With the target at disk0 and the install stick at disk1, mount the stick
and use its separate root image:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mkdir -p /stick
mount -t exfat /dev/disk1p2 /stick
rach2-install --console term --update /stick/rach2-metal-disk.ext2 /dev/disk0
umount /stick
.Ed
Save the sectors the procedure will overwrite before running the update.
.Pp
Make room for a data partition after a root of 64 GiB on a disk that has
none, from recovery:
.Dl rach2-install --data /dev/disk0 64G
The space reserved for the boot and recovery copies does not cover a
backup of a grown root.
.Pp
Move a disk installed before the kernel slots grew to the larger slots,
from recovery, and look at the result:
.Dl rach2-install --slots /dev/disk0
.Dl bootctl --target /dev/disk0p1 status
The RAM root has no room for the 1 GiB update image.
